  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Housing grinds back toward trend by FY2028-29 on demographic undersupply; lumber normalises, OSB stays mediocre, Monticello EWP starts in H1 2027, and Strategic Land Solutions holds ~$450M EBITDA with Climate Solutions growing. EBITDA margin recovers from ~18.5% to a 23.5% mid-cycle level -- about half of management's $1.5B incremental-EBITDA roadmap. Capex tapers from the Monticello peak to ~6% of sales.",
    "bull": "Mortgage rates fall and single-family starts re-accelerate from 2027; lumber and EWP realizations reset higher, OSB oversupply clears, and the natural-climate pipeline (carbon credits, biocarbon with Aymium, CCS leasing, solar/wind, data-centre sites) scales. Most of the $1.5B roadmap lands, margin reaches 28%, and the market capitalises the timberland-backed flow at a higher exit multiple.",
    "bear": "Affordability keeps starts near 1.4M through the decade, OSB overcapacity proves structural, lumber rallies are curtailment-driven and fade, and SLS earnings stay lumpy because large conservation easements do not recur. Margin stalls near 19%, leverage stays ~4x EBITDA, and the base dividend is funded but buybacks stop."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "Unlevered mid-cycle owner-FCF DCF. The latest owner FCF (~-$6M) is a housing-trough, post-interest number and is not projected; flows are rebuilt from a revenue path and a component margin build that recovers to a mid-cycle EBITDA margin. Net debt is deducted once. The REIT status shows up as a low cash tax rate on the timber half of the flow, not as a switch to AFFO-at-CoE capitalisation.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Mid-cycle margin assumption (23.5% base): each 1pp of terminal EBITDA margin is worth roughly $1/share.",
      "Terminal multiple: the timberland asset base argues for more than a cyclical 12-15x; the cap chosen (16x base) carries a large share of value.",
      "Timberland is carried at historical cost; a private-market acreage NAV may exceed this flow-based value, and the DCF does not capture it as a separate asset.",
      "Lumpy SLS land/conservation sales can swing any single year by $50-100M of EBITDA.",
      "Capex history after FY2018 comes from XBRL tags outside the CSV (PropertyPlantAndEquipmentAdditions + PaymentsForCapitalImprovements); if reforestation is double-counted in the first tag, capex is overstated by ~0.7% of sales."
    ]
  },
