    "wacc_rationale": "USD build. Risk-free ~4.3% (10y UST), ERP 5.0%, beta ~1.0 -> cost of equity ~9.3%. Pre-tax cost of debt ~4.8% (weighted-average coupon cut >130bp since 2022) with little tax shield because most interest sits at the REIT level. Market weights ~72% equity / 28% debt -> WACC ~8.0%. Same WACC in every scenario: housing-cycle risk is charged in the scenario flows and weights, not via a discount-rate premium (adaptations 7f).",
    "lease_treatment": "US GAAP ASC 842: operating lease cost already sits inside operating expense and therefore inside EBITDA; the small finance-lease balance is inside total_debt. One treatment, no adjustment.",
    "nci_treatment": "No material non-controlling interest.",
    "notes": "MODEL CHOICE: owner-FCF component DCF (unlevered, mid-cycle) with the cyclical adaptations, NOT REIT AFFO capitalisation at cost of equity. The routing table's REIT row is for IAS-40 fair-value property trusts whose flow is a post-interest AFFO; WY is a historical-cost timber REIT whose largest segment (Wood Products, ~70% of sales) is a taxable-REIT-subsidiary commodity manufacturer. EBITDA, capex and net debt all mean what the engine assumes, so the business straddles the 'REIT' and 'cyclical operating company' rows and the operating route is the one whose inputs exist. Flows are pre-interest; net debt ($5,303M debt less $527M cash, 30 Jun 2026) is deducted once in the bridge -- never capitalise FAD at CoE and also deduct debt. CAPEX: the CSV CapEx/FCF columns are blank from FY2019 (XBRL tag change); capex here is us-gaap:PropertyPlantAndEquipmentAdditions ($474M FY2025) plus PaymentsForCapitalImprovements, the timberland reforestation line ($51M) = $525M (7.6% of sales). Timberland acquisitions ($469M FY2025) are treated as land-portfolio recycling funded by divestitures and excluded from both capex and revenue growth. Capex steady-state below D&A is deliberate: ~40% of D&A is timber depletion, whose replacement is reforestation, already in capex. FY0 OWNER FCF: OCF $562M - capex $525M - SBC $43M = -$6M; that figure is post-interest ($272M paid) and FY2025 is a housing-trough year (EBITDA margin 18.0% vs ~25% 2017-2025 average), so the projection is built from components on a mid-cycle margin path, not from last_fcf. Cash tax: REIT-level timber income untaxed; TRS (Wood Products) taxed -- cash taxes were 9% of EBIT in FY2025 and ~17-18% at the FY2021-22 peak, so the rate rises with Wood Products' share of profit. SBC: 0.6% of sales, house and street nearly coincide. Q2 2026 EBITDA includes a $71M timberland-divestiture gain; the FY2026 margin anchor strips it. Dividend ~$0.84/yr (75-80% of Adjusted FAD payout framework) is a distribution of these flows, not an addition to them.",
